 "timeline": {
  "current_model": "c-Abl is a non-receptor tyrosine kinase held in an autoinhibited state by intramolecular engagement of its N-terminal myristoyl group with the kinase domain (stabilizing SH2/SH3 docking), and is activated by displacement of this myristoyl latch, phosphotyrosine ligands, upstream kinases DNA-PK and ATM (in response to DNA damage), integrin-mediated cell adhesion, and reactive oxygen species; once active it phosphorylates a wide array of substrates\u2014including p73, TAp63, YAP (Y357), \u03b1-synuclein (Y39), PARIS (Y137), RAD51 (Y315), Dok1 (Y361), WAVE3, Lamellipodin, PARP1 (Y829), GSK3\u03b2 (Y216), TFEB, and MAVS\u2014to regulate DNA damage-induced apoptosis and cell-cycle arrest (via p53/p21), actin cytoskeletal remodeling and cell migration, autophagy-lysosomal function, innate immune signaling, and neurodegeneration; activated c-Abl is downregulated by ubiquitin-proteasome-mediated degradation, and its kinase activity is directly inhibited by F-actin binding to its C-terminal domain."
